How far does trustd actually get?

A running, checked-in trustd with a stable pid answers no request at all. That is not a startup
race and not the intermittent crash, so the question is not why it dies but how far it lives, and
its own secdebug and secinfo go to syslog, which has no sink in this container. These go to stderr,
which launchd captures in StandardErrorPath.

One line per step, because the step that matters is the one whose line never appears.

WHAT IT ANSWERED, and it relocated the bug entirely:

    CIDER_TRUSTD main entered
    CIDER_TRUSTD _SecDbServerSetup done
    CIDER_TRUSTD init step: _SecTrustStoreMigrateConfigurations
    CIDER_TRUSTD init step: SecTrustServerMigrateExceptionsResetCount
    CIDER_TRUSTD migrate: ExceptionsResetCounterUrl (new)
    CIDER_TRUSTD loc: calling mbr_uid_to_uuid for euid 282
    <nothing, ever>

trustd never reaches trustd_xpc_init, so it NEVER CREATES A LISTENER AT ALL. That is why it answers
nothing: there is nothing there to answer. The task this came from was looking for a use-after-free
in the autorelease pool at dispatch_main, and trustd never gets to dispatch_main.

It stops in a membership lookup: libinfo asks opendirectoryd to translate uid 282 into a UUID, over
an XPC pipe whose round trip has no timeout.

TWO GUESSES REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT ALONG THE WAY, both recorded because each looked convincing:

Not the dispatch workloop. trustd retargets every incoming connection onto dispatch_workloop_create,
which nothing else in this container uses and which needs kernel support we may not have. A probe
shows blocks running on a workloop and a source armed on one firing, with a plain-queue control
agreeing.

Not the uid. Asked as root for uid 282 the same call came back ENOSYS once, which looked like the
caller identity mattered, since libinfo looks the service up with XPC_PIPE_FLAG_PRIVILEGED. Asked
again as root it hung. So the uid is not the discriminator, and there is now a ten-line reproducer
outside trustd.
